  "market_context": "Uptrend on EURUSD 1-hour chart with a recent impulsive move from ~0.98700 to ~1.00450, followed by a corrective pullback; analyst uses this to illustrate how to divide retracement zones into premium (above 50%) and discount (below 50%).",
  "key_observation": "The analyst observed that pullbacks in trending markets fall into two categories: small (38.2%–50%) indicating strength, and deep (50%–78.6%) indicating weakness; the 50% level splits the retracement into premium (above) and discount (below) zones.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: Identify the most recent impulsive move on the EURUSD 1H chart (from swing low near 0.98700 to swing high near 1.00450).",
    "Step 2: Apply Fibonacci retracement tool from the start (bottom wick of impulsive move) to the end (top wick of impulsive move), generating horizontal levels at 38.2%, 50%, 61.8%, and 78.6%.",
    "Step 3: Divide the retracement zone using the 50% level: label area above 50% as 'Premium' (pink zone, ~0.99500–1.00100) and below as 'Discount' (green zone, ~0.98900–0.99500).",
    "Step 4: Explain that buying in the premium zone risks early stop-outs due to liquidity sweeps, while buying in the discount zone offers better R:R but may miss strong trends.",
    "Step 5: Note that small pullbacks (38.2%–50%) signal strong trends, whereas deep pullbacks (50%–78.6%) suggest weakening momentum."
  ],
  "outcome": null,
  "lessons": "In trending markets, the 50% Fibonacci level is a critical divider: premium zones above it are higher-risk entry areas prone to liquidity grabs; discount zones below offer better risk-reward but require confirmation of trend strength.",
